  "summary": "Round 8 correctly identified the stale-graph-mid-cycle problem, the re-planning loop risk, and the dual-client architecture gap. This round provides concrete resolutions: (1) The DependencyGraph staleness during auto() processing is a real problem but has a simple fix — rather than rebuilding the entire graph between passes, the graph should expose an update_stage(feature_id, new_stage) mutation that move_to_stage() calls after every transition, keeping the snapshot current within a cycle at O(1) cost per move; for the CLI auto() in pipeline.py (which doesn't use the server's DependencyGraph), the dependency check should be a simple standalone function that calls list_all() once at the start of process_features() and builds a local id-to-stage dict, since the CLI auto path processes one feature at a time and re-enters process_features() for each iteration anyway. (2) The re-planning loop is the most dangerous unresolved issue: if feature B depends on rejected feature A, and B is moved to plan-inbox for re-planning, the auto-planner will generate a new plan — but since depends_on still contains A's ID and is_blocked() treats 'rejected' as resolved, B will proceed to implementation with a plan that may still assume A's functionality exists. The fix is NOT to mutate depends_on automatically (that violates the 'static declaration edited only by users' principle from round 7), but to add a `dependency_notes` field to the re-planning context: when plan_invalidation_check() moves B back to plan-inbox, it should append to B's pipeline_log a structured note like {type: 'dependency_rejected', dependency_id: A.id, dependency_title: A.title, note: 'This dependency was rejected — your plan must not assume this feature exists'}, and the planning phase in phases.py should include these notes in the LLM prompt context so the planner explicitly addresses the gap. This is the same pattern already used for plan_reviews feedback — rejected plan reviews include feedback that the re-planning prompt consumes. (3) The dual-client architecture question has a clear answer from the code: ALL mutations go through PipelineServer._dispatch_command() regardless of source. The TUI calls PipelineClient.send_command() which sends over Unix socket to PipelineServer. The WebUI calls Go API handlers which send over WebSocket to PipelineServer._handle_golang_message() which calls _dispatch_command(). Both paths converge at the same handler. So adding edit_depends_on requires: (a) a new _cmd_edit_depends_on handler in server.py that validates the input (cycle detection, same-board restriction, no self-reference), mutates the feature, saves, and broadcasts; (b) a PipelineClient.edit_depends_on() convenience method mirroring the existing edit_description() pattern; (c) a Go API endpoint serveEditDependsOn() following the exact same request/response pattern as serveMoveFeature() — create request ID, send WebSocket message, wait for result; (d) a TUI edit handler _handle_edit_depends_on() that calls PipelineClient.edit_depends_on(). The cycle detection belongs in the server handler (_cmd_edit_depends_on), not in FeatureFile, because it requires the full feature graph — the handler already has access to list_all() and can build the adjacency list on demand. (4) One issue no previous round has addressed: the FeatureSummary in hub.go is a struct that maps 1:1 to the state broadcast JSON. Adding depends_on and a computed blocked field requires updating both the Python _build_golang_state_message() (which constructs the feature dict) and the Go FeatureSummary struct (which deserializes it). The blocked field should be computed server-side in Python during broadcast construction (since Python owns the feature graph), not computed client-side in Go or JavaScript — this keeps the dependency logic in one place and means the WebUI just reads a boolean. (5) For the conditional persistence question: agree with round 6 that depends_on should only be written to JSON when non-empty, but this creates an asymmetry — save() must use a conditional like `if self._data.get('depends_on'): data['depends_on'] = self._data['depends_on']`, and the property getter must use .get('depends_on', []). This is the same pattern already used for optional fields like done_script and ideation_prompt in the existing codebase."
